### What are the two method sets?

The Queue interface defines six methods organized into three operations, each with a throwing and a special-value variant:

| Operation | Throws exception | Returns special value |
|---|---|---|
| Insert | `add(e)` → IllegalStateException if full | `offer(e)` → false if full |
| Remove | `remove()` → NoSuchElementException if empty | `poll()` → null if empty |
| Examine | `element()` → NoSuchElementException if empty | `peek()` → null if empty |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.ArrayBlockingQueue;

public class QueueMethodsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Bounded queue for demonstration
        Queue<String> queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(2);

        // === INSERT ===
        queue.add("TXN-001");    // OK
        queue.add("TXN-002");    // OK – queue is now full

        // add() on full queue: throws
        try {
            queue.add("TXN-003");
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println("add() on full: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        // offer() on full queue: returns false
        boolean accepted = queue.offer("TXN-003");
        System.out.println("offer() on full: " + accepted);

        // === REMOVE ===
        queue.poll(); // TXN-001
        queue.poll(); // TXN-002 – queue is now empty

        // remove() on empty queue: throws
        try {
            queue.remove();
        } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {
            System.out.println("remove() on empty: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }

        // poll() on empty queue: returns null
        String result = queue.poll();
        System.out.println("poll() on empty: " + result);

        // === EXAMINE ===
        // element() on empty: throws
        try {
            queue.element();
        } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {
            System.out.println("element() on empty: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }

        // peek() on empty: returns null
        String peeked = queue.peek();
        System.out.println("peek() on empty: " + peeked);
    }
}
```

```text
add() on full: Queue full
offer() on full: false
remove() on empty: NoSuchElementException
poll() on empty: null
element() on empty: NoSuchElementException
peek() on empty: null
```

### When do you use throwing methods vs special-value methods?

Use **special-value methods** (offer/poll/peek) when:
- Empty or full is a normal runtime condition – not a bug.
- You are implementing a consumer loop that checks for available items.
- You are using a bounded queue where the full condition is expected.
- You are in a time-sensitive path where exceptions are expensive.

Use **throwing methods** (add/remove/element) when:
- Failure indicates a programming error or violated invariant.
- The queue should never be empty or full at this point in the code.
- You want to fail fast with a clear exception message.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class MethodChoiceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Consumer loop: poll() is correct (empty = normal)
        Queue<String> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer("TXN-001");
        queue.offer("TXN-002");

        String txn;
        while ((txn = queue.poll()) != null) {
            System.out.println("Processing: " + txn);
        }
        System.out.println("Queue drained");

        // Bounded queue: offer() is correct (full = normal back-pressure)
        BlockingQueue<String> bounded = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(2);
        bounded.offer("A");
        bounded.offer("B");
        boolean added = bounded.offer("C"); // false – full, no exception
        System.out.println("Offered to full queue: " + added);

        // Invariant check: element() when you EXPECT the queue is not empty
        Queue<String> mustNotBeEmpty = new ArrayDeque<>();
        mustNotBeEmpty.add("REQUIRED");
        String head = mustNotBeEmpty.element(); // throws if empty = BUG
        System.out.println("Head: " + head);
    }
}
```

```text
Processing: TXN-001
Processing: TXN-002
Queue drained
Offered to full queue: false
Head: REQUIRED
```

### What does BlockingQueue add?

BlockingQueue extends Queue with two additional method sets:

| Operation | Throws | Special value | Blocks | Timed |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Insert | add(e) | offer(e) | put(e) | offer(e, time, unit) |
| Remove | remove() | poll() | take() | poll(time, unit) |
| Examine | element() | peek() | N/A | N/A |

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class BlockingMethodsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws InterruptedException {
        BlockingQueue<String> queue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(2);

        // put() blocks if full
        queue.put("TXN-001"); // OK
        queue.put("TXN-002"); // OK – now full
        // queue.put("TXN-003"); // would BLOCK until space is available

        // take() blocks if empty
        String txn = queue.take(); // TXN-001
        System.out.println("Taken: " + txn);

        // Timed: offer with timeout
        boolean added = queue.offer("TXN-003", 1, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        System.out.println("Timed offer: " + added);

        // Timed: poll with timeout
        String polled = queue.poll(1, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        System.out.println("Timed poll: " + polled);
    }
}
```

```text
Taken: TXN-001
Timed offer: true
Timed poll: TXN-002
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake 1: Using add() on a bounded queue

```java
// WRONG – add() throws when full
BlockingQueue<String> bounded = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(2);
bounded.add("A");
bounded.add("B");
bounded.add("C"); // IllegalStateException!

// CORRECT – offer() returns false
bounded.offer("C"); // false, no exception

// OR – put() blocks until space available
bounded.put("C"); // waits (needs another thread to take)
```

#### Mistake 2: Using poll() with null elements

```java
// If your queue allows null elements and you use poll():
Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>();
queue.offer(null);
String result = queue.poll(); // null – but was this empty or the null element?
// Ambiguous! That is why BlockingQueue does NOT allow null elements.
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Null ambiguity.** `poll()` and `peek()` return null to signal "empty." If the queue contains null elements, there is no way to distinguish "empty" from "null element." This is why most concurrent queue implementations reject null.

- **Exception cost.** Throwing and catching exceptions is expensive (~10x slower than a return statement). In a high-throughput consumer loop, using `remove()` on an empty queue and catching `NoSuchElementException` wastes CPU. Use `poll()` instead.

- **Deque extends Queue.** Deque provides all Queue methods plus double-ended variants: `offerFirst`/`offerLast`, `pollFirst`/`pollLast`, `peekFirst`/`peekLast`.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is the difference between add() and offer()?" – add throws, offer returns false.
- "What is the difference between poll() and take()?" – poll returns null, take blocks.
- "Why does BlockingQueue not allow null?" – null is the special value returned by poll/peek for empty.
- "When would you use add() over offer()?" – When failure means a programming bug.
